; max31856_spi_slave.pio -- PIO SPI *slave* engines for the MAX31856
; emulation buses. docs/DESIGN_NOTES.md section 3.2.1 is the authoritative design for
; the RX/TX state machine split, mode-1 SPI and the multi-CS-on-bus-A /
; single-CS-on-bus-B asymmetry -- but NOT for the first-byte latency budget:
; that section's 1.6us figure was shown to be wrong by ~8x in
; docs/SPI_ACCESS_AUDIT.md section 6 item 2, and the DMA-fed "Plan B" that
; replaced it is described below and in max31856_pio_engine.h. This file is
; the PIO half; the C glue that loads/configures/drives these programs lives
; in max31856_pio_engine.c right next to this file.
;
; ============================================================================
; MODE-1 EDGE RESOLUTION (see the RX program comment below for the full
; derivation): the two real masters that will ever clock this fixture agree
; with each other and with the datasheet, so the edges are settled from code
; + datasheet alone, no bench measurement required.
;   - KilnFW (ESP32-S3): MAX31856.c `#define MAX31856_SPI_MODE 1`, passed as
;     `spi_device_interface_config_t.mode` in MAX31856_attach() -- ESP-IDF
;     mode 1 == CPOL=0, CPHA=1.
;   - SaftyFW (RP2040): spi_owner.c `spi_set_format(spi0, 8, SPI_CPOL_0,
;     SPI_CPHA_1, SPI_MSB_FIRST)` -- explicit CPOL=0, CPHA=1, same as KilnFW.
;   - KilnFW/docs/HARDWARE.md's SPI bus section: "The thermocouple parts run
;     SPI mode 1 at up to 5 MHz."
;   - MAX31856 datasheet (hardware/datasheets/.../MAX31856.pdf), Table 5
;     "Serial Interface Function", CPOL=0 rows (page 15; "Note: CPHA bit
;     polarity must be set to 1" for the whole table, i.e. mode 1 is the only
;     mode the table describes): SDI ("Data bit latch", i.e. the *slave's*
;     input sample point) happens on "SCLK falling"; SDO ("Next data bit
;     shift", i.e. the *slave's* output/shift point) happens on "SCLK
;     rising". This is the MAX31856 itself telling you, as a slave device,
;     which edge it samples/shifts on -- exactly the role this PIO program
;     plays.
; So: this emulator, acting as the slave, must SAMPLE MOSI on the FALLING
; edge and SHIFT MISO out on the RISING edge. The RX/TX programs below were
; originally written backwards (sampled on rising, shifted on falling --
; textbook mode 0, not mode 1, despite the mode-1 label), per the file's
; former ambiguity note; that has now been corrected to match the three
; sources above, which all agree.
; ============================================================================
; BUILD-VERIFIED, NOT HARDWARE-TIMING-VERIFIED. There is no Pico attached in
; this environment. pioasm (run automatically by pico_generate_pio_header()
; at build time) validates that these programs assemble and fit PIO program
; memory; it does not itself drive a real ESP32-S3/RP2040 master, so the
; edge choices above -- though derived from the masters' own SPI config and
; the datasheet's own slave-timing table, not guessed -- are still worth a
; Saleae confirmation on first bench contact (PLAN.md section 10 M-A's exit
; criterion) before fully trusting the fixture.
; ============================================================================
;
; ============================================================================
; PLAN B (DMA-fed). docs/SPI_ACCESS_AUDIT.md section 6 item 2 corrected the
; first-byte budget from "one byte-time" to "half an SCLK period" -- ~125 ns
; at 4 MHz -- because the address byte's VALUE is only known after its last
; bit is latched on falling edge 8, while the first response bit must be on
; MISO by rising edge 9. No RP2040 ISR does that, so the CPU is out of the
; response path entirely and the RX program below does one extra job: it
; assembles the address byte directly into a POINTER INTO THE RESPONSE IMAGE
; (src/sim/max31856_resp_image.h), which a DMA control channel hands to a data
; channel's read-address trigger. See max31856_pio_engine.c's "PLAN B DATA
; FLOW" comment for the full chain and its cycle budget.
; ============================================================================
;
; --- Pin-mapping contract every program below assumes (enforced by the C
; glue's sm_config_set_*_pins calls in max31856_pio_engine.c, not by pioasm
; itself -- get this wrong in the .c file and these programs silently watch
; the wrong pin) ---
;
;   max31856_spi_rx      (one instance per emulated channel, shared program):
;     IN_BASE  = MOSI gpio            -- `in pins, 1` samples exactly MOSI
;     JMP_PIN  = this channel's own CS gpio (absolute gpio, independent of
;                IN_BASE -- active LOW: jmp_pin reads pin state directly,
;                `jmp pin, target` jumps when the pin is HIGH i.e. CS
;                deasserted)
;     SCLK reached via offset 31 from IN_BASE, i.e. the C glue must place
;     SCLK at gpio (MOSI_gpio - 1) so (SCLK - MOSI) mod 32 == 31. This only
;     works because PIO pin-index arithmetic always wraps mod 32 regardless
;     of how many real GPIOs exist (RP2040 exposes far fewer than 32, so the
;     wrap is a deliberate, standard trick, not an accident). The concrete
;     numbers live in spi_emu_a.c / spi_emu_b.c's pin blocks, and
;     max31856_pio_engine_init() REJECTS a config that does not satisfy the
;     arithmetic rather than trusting the call site to have read this.
;
;   max31856_spi_tx_a    (bus A: 3 CS lines share one physical MISO):
;     IN_BASE       = first of 3 *consecutive* CS gpios (CS0,CS1,CS2) --
;                     `in pins, 3` reads all three at once into a single
;                     idle/active test word.
;     OUT_BASE/SET_BASE = MISO gpio  -- `out pins,1` drives MISO's value,
;                     `set pindirs,n` drives MISO's output-enable (tri-state
;                     control -- DESIGN_NOTES.md 3.2.1: "MISO pin is tri-stated...
;                     whenever no CS is low, since three emulated chips share
;                     one physical MISO on bus A").
;     SCLK reached via offset 29 from IN_BASE (CS0 - SCLK == 3 in the chosen
;     provisional pinout, so (SCLK - CS0) mod 32 == 29).
;
;   max31856_spi_tx_b    (bus B: single CS, tri-stating kept for symmetry
;     even though PLAN.md notes it is "optional/simpler" there -- costs
;     nothing and means one C code path instead of two):
;     With only one CS line, "is CS asserted" collapses to a single pin
;     level, so this program uses `jmp pin` instead of tx_a's
;     in/mov/mov/jmp idiom (see the program's own header comment for why):
;       IN_BASE = this channel's CS gpio, used ONLY by `wait ... pin 29`
;                 below -- unchanged from before this program was shrunk.
;       JMP_PIN = the SAME CS gpio, but a SEPARATE config field
;                 (sm_config_set_jmp_pin(), absolute gpio, active LOW same
;                 sense as the RX program's JMP_PIN above) that the C glue
;                 must set explicitly -- there is no default that makes this
;                 "just work", unlike the old idiom's shift-direction trap.
;     SCLK offset is still 29 by construction (same CS-to-SCLK gpio spacing
;     reused on bus B, see the C glue) and is unaffected by JMP_PIN, since
;     `wait ... pin N` always resolves against IN_BASE, never JMP_PIN.
;
; --- Shift-direction / byte-justification contract (also enforced by the C
; glue, called out here so the two files can be cross-checked) ---
;   RX: sm_config_set_in_shift(shift_right=false, autopush=true,
;       threshold=10). TEN, not eight -- this is the Plan B change and the C
;       glue must match it exactly -- max31856_pio_engine.c sets it in
;       max31856_pio_engine_init()'s RX loop, and config_is_sane() there
;       checks the pin arithmetic these offsets depend on.
;       Each byte is assembled as:
;           mov isr, x        ; ISR <- image_base >> 10, shift counter <- 0
;                             ;   (MOV to ISR resets the input shift counter,
;                             ;    RP2040 datasheet section 3.4.5 "MOV")
;           8 x in pins, 1    ; ISR = (base>>10)<<8 | byte
;           in null, 2        ; ISR = (base>>10)<<10 | byte<<2 = base | byte*4
;                             ;   and the 10th shifted bit fires autopush.
;       So every pushed FIFO word is a WORD-ALIGNED POINTER INTO THE 256-ENTRY
;       RESPONSE IMAGE, not a bare byte:
;           - the first word of a transaction is consumed by the SNIFF DMA
;             channel and ends up in the data channel's read-address trigger,
;             with no CPU involvement and no arithmetic anywhere;
;           - every later word is consumed by the CPU, which recovers the
;             data byte with `(word >> 2) & 0xFF`.
;       Preloading the base on EVERY byte (not just the first) is deliberate:
;       it means the program has no "is this the address byte" state to get
;       wrong, so a missed CS-rise edge can never mis-frame the next
;       transaction -- it just re-enters byte_start with a correct pointer.
;       Which word is the address byte is decided entirely by the SNIFF
;       channel's transfer count of 1, i.e. by the DMA, not by the PIO.
;       The image base MUST be 1024-byte aligned for the OR-into-the-top-bits
;       trick to be an OR at all; max31856_pio_engine_publish_image() checks
;       that at run time and refuses a misaligned image.
;   TX (IN side -- the CS idle/active test, NOT a byte stream) -- TX_A ONLY
;     as of the tx_b `jmp pin` rewrite (tx_b has no `in` instruction any
;     more, see its own program comment; this paragraph is kept because
;     tx_a is unchanged and still depends on it):
;       sm_config_set_in_shift(shift_right=false, autopush=false, 32).
;       MANDATORY for tx_a. The `in pins, N` / `mov x, isr` / `jmp x!=y`
;       sequence in tx_a compares against a `set`-loaded constant (7, all 3
;       CS lines idle-high). Only shifting LEFT lands the N sampled CS bits
;       in ISR[N-1:0] where that constant means something; the pico-sdk
;       DEFAULT is shift-RIGHT, which parks them at ISR[31:32-N]
;       (0xE0000000 for an idle bus A) so the compare never matches, MISO is
;       never tri-stated, and the SM never returns to poll_idle. This was a
;       real shipped bug -- see docs/SPI_ACCESS_AUDIT.md D1. The C glue
;       (max31856_pio_engine.c) sets this once for a `pio_sm_config` shared
;       by whichever TX program variant gets loaded; it is harmless-unused
;       for tx_b rather than removed, since removing it would need a
;       per-program branch for zero behavioural gain.
;   TX: sm_config_set_out_shift(shift_right=false, autopull=true,
;       threshold=8). OUT shifts LEFT and emits the current MSB of a 32-bit
;       OSR each time, so the C side must LEFT-JUSTIFY each response byte
;       before pushing it: `pio_sm_put(pio, sm_tx, (uint32_t)byte << 24)`.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; RX: samples MOSI on SCLK's FALLING edge, one whole byte at a time
; (autopush at the 10-bit threshold: 8 sampled bits plus 2 zero bits of
; word-scaling -- see the shift-direction contract above), gated by this
; channel's own CS line. Falls back to a clean byte boundary whenever CS is
; seen high, whether that is between transactions or an out-of-protocol
; mid-byte deassert -- a well-behaved master never does the latter, but this
; program does not assume it.
;
; MODE-1 EDGE DERIVATION (see file header for the full source list -- this
; is the short version, worked from first principles so it's checkable):
; mode 1 is CPOL=0 (idle low), CPHA=1. CPHA=1 means: the leading edge of
; each clock cycle is when the *transmitting* side (master's MOSI driver,
; slave's MISO driver) puts out its next bit, and the trailing edge is when
; the *receiving* side latches it. For CPOL=0 the leading edge of a cycle is
; the RISING edge and the trailing edge is the FALLING edge. That rule
; applies identically to both ends of the same shared clock -- master and
; slave are not on opposite edges of each other, they both key off the same
; leading/trailing split. So: the master drives a new MOSI bit on SCLK's
; RISING edge, and this slave (the emulator) must sample it on the
; following FALLING edge, once it has settled. This exactly matches the
; MAX31856 datasheet's own Table 5 (CPOL=0 row): "Data bit latch" (SDI
; sample) on "SCLK falling". This program used to sample on the rising edge
; (textbook mode 0); it has been corrected to sample on falling to actually
; be mode 1.
;
; PLAN B ADDITION: this SM's own TX FIFO carries the response-image base
; (>> 10) published by the CPU, and X caches it so `pull noblock` on an empty
; FIFO re-supplies it (RP2040: a non-blocking PULL on an empty TX FIFO
; behaves as MOV OSR, X). The CPU republishes by a single 32-bit
; pio_sm_put() -- one atomic store -- which is what makes the double-banked
; response image tear-proof: the SM adopts a new bank only at `idle`, i.e.
; only while CS is high, so a burst in flight keeps reading the bank it
; started on. Y is the per-byte bit counter, so both scratch registers are
; spoken for and the program has no spare state; keep it that way.
.program max31856_spi_rx
idle:
    pull noblock               ; adopt a newly published image base, else re-supply the cached one from X
    mov x, osr                  ; X = image_base >> 10 (the cache `pull noblock` falls back to)
    jmp pin, idle                ; CS (this SM's jmp_pin) HIGH == deasserted -> keep polling, keep the bank fresh
byte_start:
    mov isr, x                  ; preload the image base; also resets the input shift counter (see header)
    set y, 7                     ; 8 bits per byte: one `in` below plus 7 loop passes
bit_loop:
    wait 1 pin 31                 ; SCLK rising edge (offset 31 == SCLK, wraparound from IN_BASE=MOSI, see header) -- mode-1 leading edge: this is when the master drives its next MOSI bit, NOT our sample point
    wait 0 pin 31                  ; SCLK falling edge -- mode-1 trailing edge: MOSI has settled, this IS the sample point
    in pins, 1                    ; sample MOSI into ISR, MSB first
    jmp pin, idle                  ; CS deasserted (mid-byte or at a boundary) -> abandon this byte and re-poll
    jmp y--, bit_loop
    in null, 2                      ; shift in the 2 low zero bits -> ISR is now image_base | (byte * 4), and the 10-bit autopush threshold fires here
    jmp byte_start                   ; next byte in the same CS window; no per-byte CS wait, framing comes from the bit count

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; TX (bus A variant): shared MISO shifter for all 3 emulated channels on the
; ESP-side bus. Tri-states MISO (pindir=0, Hi-Z) whenever all 3 CS lines read
; high; drives it (pindir=1) and shifts a byte out per SCLK cycle whenever
; any one of them is low. Complementary edge to the RX program above: shifts
; (changes MISO) on SCLK's RISING edge -- the mode-1 leading edge, see the RX
; program's derivation -- and holds through the FALLING edge, so the
; master's own sample point (the trailing/falling edge, per the MAX31856
; datasheet Table 5 CPOL=0 "SDO ... SCLK rising" row read from the slave's
; output side) sees a settled bit rather than one still transitioning.
.program max31856_spi_tx_a
    set y, 7                   ; compare constant: 0b111 == all 3 CS lines idle-high
poll_idle:
    in pins, 3                  ; sample CS0,CS1,CS2 (IN_BASE = CS0) into ISR
    mov x, isr
    mov isr, null                ; ISR is scratch here (not a byte stream) -- clear it back out
    jmp x!=y, active
    set pindirs, 0                ; tri-state MISO -- no channel selected
    jmp poll_idle
active:
    set pindirs, 1                 ; drive MISO -- at least one CS asserted
byte_loop:
    wait 1 pin 29                   ; SCLK rising edge (mode-1 leading edge) -> safe to change MISO
    out pins, 1                      ; shift next bit out; autopull refills the OSR from the TX FIFO at the 8-bit threshold, BLOCKING (stalling this SM) if the FIFO is empty. Under Plan B the FIFO is fed by the DMA data channel, so this stall is STRUCTURAL at the start of every transaction (nothing can exist there until the address byte has been decoded) and is NOT what the underrun counter watches -- max31856_pio_engine.c counts underruns from the DMA's own delivered-byte count instead; see that file's stats comment
    wait 0 pin 29                     ; SCLK falling edge (mode-1 trailing edge) -- hold the bit steady through the master's sample window
    in pins, 3                         ; re-check CS state every bit (cheaper than a separate SM, and CS can legitimately deassert mid-byte on an aborted transaction)
    mov x, isr
    mov isr, null
    jmp x!=y, byte_loop
    set pindirs, 0                       ; all CS lines rose -> tri-state again
    jmp poll_idle

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; TX (bus B variant): bus B has exactly ONE CS line, which means the
; idle/active test doesn't need tx_a's "sample N pins into the ISR, stash in
; X, clear the ISR, compare against a SET-loaded constant" idiom at all --
; that idiom exists only to turn a multi-bit sample into a single comparable
; word. With one CS bit, "is CS asserted" IS a single pin's level, which is
; exactly what the dedicated `jmp pin, <target>` instruction tests in ONE
; word (branches when the configured JMP pin reads HIGH). CS is idle-high on
; this bus like every other, so `jmp pin, idle_state` is a direct substitute
; for the old 4-instruction "in/mov/mov/jmp" block, both places it appeared
; (the idle poll and the mid-byte CS re-check). That drops this program from
; 17 words to 10, which is what frees the PIO1 program-memory room
; ct_wave_i2s_out needs (docs/DESIGN_NOTES.md section 3.2.1 / this file's TX pin-mapping
; contract above records the follow-on C-side change: the tx SM's JMP pin is
; now explicitly configured via sm_config_set_jmp_pin(), separately from
; IN_BASE, in max31856_pio_engine.c).
;
; IMPORTANT -- two separate pin-config fields, do not confuse them:
;   - IN_BASE (sm_config_set_in_pins) is UNCHANGED: still this channel's CS
;     gpio, still what `wait 1/0 pin 29` resolves its offset-29 SCLK index
;     against (see the file's pin-mapping contract above). This program has
;     no `in` instruction any more, so IN_BASE only matters for the two
;     `wait ... pin 29` lines below.
;   - JMP_PIN (sm_config_set_jmp_pin) is a DIFFERENT config field that takes
;     an ABSOLUTE gpio number (not an IN_BASE-relative offset). It must be
;     set to this same CS gpio explicitly on the C side, or `jmp pin` tests
;     the wrong line and this SM never tri-states MISO or notices CS rise --
;     exactly D1's failure shape (SPI_ACCESS_AUDIT.md), just on a different
;     instruction. sm_config_set_jmp_pin() has no useful default; leaving it
;     unset is not a "does nothing" mistake the way the old shift-direction
;     default was, it is undefined which pin gets tested.
;
; Because this program issues no `in` instruction, the ISR/IN-shift-direction
; contract in this file's header (the "MANDATORY... shift LEFT" paragraph)
; no longer applies to this program -- it still applies to tx_a, which is
; unchanged and still uses the old idiom. max31856_pio_engine.c's shared
; sm_config_set_in_shift() call is therefore dead weight for this SM
; specifically; it is left in place because it is still load-bearing for
; tx_a's shared config object and removing it would need a per-program branch
; for zero behavioural gain (see that file's comment at the call site).
.program max31856_spi_tx_b
poll_idle:
    jmp pin, idle_state          ; CS (JMP_PIN, absolute gpio, see above) HIGH == deasserted -> stay idle
    jmp active                    ; CS LOW -> a transaction is starting
idle_state:
    set pindirs, 0                 ; tri-state MISO -- no CS asserted
    jmp poll_idle
active:
    set pindirs, 1                  ; drive MISO -- CS asserted
byte_loop:
    wait 1 pin 29                     ; SCLK rising edge (mode-1 leading edge; offset-29 CS-to-SCLK spacing off IN_BASE, unchanged -- see header)
    out pins, 1                        ; shift next bit out; autopull refills the OSR from the TX FIFO at the 8-bit threshold, blocking if empty (same structural first-transaction stall as tx_a, see that program's byte_loop comment)
    wait 0 pin 29                       ; SCLK falling edge (mode-1 trailing/sample edge) -- hold the bit steady through the master's sample window
    jmp pin, idle_state                  ; CS released (mid-byte or at a boundary) -> tri-state, re-poll
    jmp byte_loop
